  {
    "name": "Windjammer",
    "lat": 12.2202758,
    "lon": -68.3753404,
    "osm_id": 2625769975,
    "osm_type": "node",
    "depth": 55,
    "site_type": "wreck",
    "entry_type": "boat",
    "difficulty": "Technical",
    "tags": {
      "name": "Windjammer",
      "sport": "scuba_diving",
      "source": "osm_extended",
      "addedBy": "extended_overpass",
      "validated": "true",
      "validation_source": "dreamwrecks.com,divers-guide.com,numa.net,mass.cultureelerfgoed.nl",
      "last_qa_date": "2026-07-19",
      "qa_note": "Deep re-audit 2026-07-19 found MAJOR conflicts across independent sources on cause of sinking, casualties, tonnage, and depth. Netherlands Cultural Heritage maritime-archaeology database (mass.cultureelerfgoed.nl) says: 1278 tons, storm shifted loose tar cargo causing list, anchor failed, grounded on reef 7 Dec 1912, no casualties mentioned, wreck at 60m with a mast visible at 12m. NUMA (numa.net) says: 1,315 tons, 239ft, ran aground in a storm then cargo (asphalt) caught fire from a kerosene lamp causing explosions, 4 crew died/28 survived, wreck at 140-180ft (43-55m). DreamWrecks says: 378 tons, initial grounding 7 Dec 1905, then a separate hurricane in Dec 1912 capsized her; a historian (Maurice Coutts) theorized the captain deliberately grounded the aging ship; 2 people died later during salvage when a mast fell; wreck at ~200ft (61m) with one mast in 30ft (9m) of water. Markdown rewritten to present only the facts common to all sources (built 1874, Glasgow, Barclay Curle & Co; wrecked on a Bonaire reef in a storm in 1912) and to explicitly flag the conflicting tonnage/cause/casualty/depth claims rather than picking one. Removed prior unsupported claim that 'both masts still stand upright' -- no current source confirms standing masts at 35-40m; sources instead describe masts lying on the bottom or a mast section in much shallower water (9-12m)."
    }
  },
